From abec462078bd7a038058e24bea1a39ab206f9baf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Adrien Delmas <adrien@adriendelmas.com>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 19:54:38 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 048/147] hydra: standard_surface dispersion now reaches the
 principled bsdf

Cycles just gained dispersion on its principled bsdf (0038, ported from Blender
PR 162041), but the MaterialX translation did not pass it on yet -
`transmission_dispersion` remained in the list of inputs without an equivalent.

MaterialX has only one slider for this, which reads directly as Cycles' Abbe
number - both divide by it the same way (kernel/svm/closure.h) and treat 0 as
"no dispersion", the division by zero canceling the effect. Mapped as is onto
transmission_dispersion_abbe_number.

Cycles separately carries a scale factor that MaterialX does not have. Since
the real on/off switch lies on that factor (not on the Abbe number), it is set
to 1 so that `transmission_dispersion` is the only thing that turns dispersion
on or off.

A first version set only that factor, assuming that a network never touching
`transmission_dispersion` would receive 0 by default - MaterialX's own default
for this input. Wrong: Hydra only passes the parameters that are realized, and
Cycles' default for the Abbe number is 20, not 0. Any non-zero `transmission`
material that had never touched dispersion therefore inherited some, without
having asked for it - a regression caught on the bench before any commit (97
nodes compared, cycles_math and 96 others that have nothing to do with
MaterialX changed by several percent of pixels, traced back to mxGlass in the
shared bench scene). Fixed by also setting the Abbe number to 0 - MaterialX's
true input default - overridden by the normal mapping as soon as the network
actually writes it.

Verified: an explicit test (transmission_dispersion=3, MaterialX material
exported through a real Solaris Material Library) now renders visibly different
from a variant without dispersion. The 97-node bench is again identical to the
reference to the pixel.

Side note, unrelated and not investigated: a hand-placed USD Sphere lost its
material binding (fell back to default_surface) in one of the throwaway test
scenarios of this investigation - to check some day, perhaps an artifact of
bypassing Houdini's usual authoring pipeline rather than a real delegate bug.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/hydra/material.cpp | 21 ++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 20 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/hydra/material.cpp b/src/hydra/material.cpp
index 4c1c0bc4..6b9bf9da 100644
--- a/src/hydra/material.cpp
+++ b/src/hydra/material.cpp
@@ -295,7 +295,7 @@ class MtlxFacOrColor : public UsdToCyclesMapping {
  *  - base / specular / transmission_color: weights and tints Cycles folds into
  *    other inputs,
  *  - subsurface_color: Cycles derives it from base_color,
- *  - transmission_extra_roughness, transmission_dispersion. */
+ *  - transmission_extra_roughness. */
 UsdToCyclesMapping MakeMtlxStandardSurface();
 
 const UsdToCyclesMapping MtlxStandardSurface = MakeMtlxStandardSurface();
@@ -322,6 +322,14 @@ UsdToCyclesMapping MakeMtlxStandardSurface()
         {TfToken("specular_anisotropy"), ustring("anisotropic")},
         {TfToken("specular_rotation"), ustring("anisotropic_rotation")},
         {TfToken("transmission"), ustring("transmission_weight")},
+        /* MaterialX's single dispersion slider reads as Cycles' Abbe number
+         * directly - both use it the same way (kernel/svm/closure.h divides
+         * the dispersion scale by it), and both treat 0 as "no dispersion",
+         * since dividing by zero zeroes the effect out. Cycles' own enable
+         * switch is the scale, not the Abbe number, and defaults to off; it
+         * is pinned to 1 below so the Abbe number is the only thing left to
+         * drive it, matching a slider MaterialX doesn't have. */
+        {TfToken("transmission_dispersion"), ustring("transmission_dispersion_abbe_number")},
         {TfToken("subsurface"), ustring("subsurface_weight")},
         {TfToken("subsurface_radius"), ustring("subsurface_radius")},
         {TfToken("subsurface_scale"), ustring("subsurface_scale")},
@@ -343,6 +351,17 @@ UsdToCyclesMapping MakeMtlxStandardSurface()
         {TfToken("normal"), ustring("normal")},
         {TfToken("tangent"), ustring("tangent")},
     }};
+  /* MaterialX has no dispersion scale of its own, so it is pinned to 1 and
+   * `transmission_dispersion` above is left as the only thing that turns
+   * dispersion on or off. But Cycles' own Abbe number default is 20, not 0 -
+   * a network that never touches `transmission_dispersion` sends no value
+   * for it at all (Hydra only carries authored parameters), so without a
+   * matching default here every glass material would pick up dispersion it
+   * never asked for. Pinning the Abbe number to 0 alongside the scale
+   * reproduces MaterialX's own default for the input, and is overridden by
+   * the mapping above wherever the network does author it. */
+  mapping.setFixedValues({{ustring("transmission_dispersion_scale"), "1"},
+                          {ustring("transmission_dispersion_abbe_number"), "0"}});
   /* The helper drives the level unconditionally, so it has to carry MaterialX's
    * own default of 1 for the case where the network never writes `specular` at
    * all - otherwise an unauthored input would be actively set to something
